    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d138-a2">

                <pb facs="0278" n="186" xml:id="pg186"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Mr.
                            Fowler</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States of America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Pekin, China</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1899-07-03"><hi rendition="#italic">July 3,
                                1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 373.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your dispatch No. 188 of
                    June 28, reporting a request for passports from Port Arthur by two disreputable
                    women, who required the passports in order to be able to continue to ply their
                    vocation at that place, and inquiring if I will authorize you to refuse them and
                    other like characters when applying for passports for such purposes.</p>
                <p>As a general rule, it would hardly do to make moral character a basis for the
                    issuance of passports, yet, in these Eastern countries, where certificates of
                    citizenship stand for so much, I shall not furnish passports to parties who are
                    known to be of the class and plying the vocation you name.</p>
                <p>When, therefore, there is no question whatever to the facts, you may refuse to
                    forward applications, and consequently to give travel certificates.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. H. Conger.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
